We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-1$ to get $x^2+6x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+7$:

$\blueD{(x-1)(x+7)=x^2+6x-7}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+6x-4}{x-1}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2+6x-7})+3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+6x-7}}{x-1}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-1)(x+7)=x^2+6x-7}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+6x-7}}{x-1}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-1)}}\blueD{(x+7)}}{\cancel{x-1}}+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\\\\
&=x+7+\dfrac{3}{x-1}
\end{align}$
